Object.assign(sensoryCases,{
 'SIT-NP4-neuroanatomy-assessment-basics-01':{
  source:'https://www.resus.org.uk/library/abcde-approach',scenario:'A previously alert inpatient now needs repeated stimulation to respond and cannot sustain attention during the neurological check. This is a new change from the documented baseline.',
  recognition:['Which interpretation should guide the immediate response?',
   ['An acute decline in responsiveness requiring urgent assessment','A new reduction in alertness can signal neurological or systemic deterioration. Compare with baseline and assess urgently.'],
   ['Normal sleepiness that requires no observations','Sleep can affect responsiveness, but a persistent new inability to maintain attention needs assessment before being labeled normal.'],
   ['A specific intracranial diagnosis established by the observation','The change establishes deterioration, not its exact cause. Airway, physiological, metabolic, and neurological assessment guide the diagnosis.'],
   ['Poor cooperation that should be managed by repeating commands louder','Communication barriers should be checked, but the new decline cannot be assumed to be voluntary.']],
  action:['What should the nurse prioritize?',
   ['Assess airway, breathing, circulation, glucose, and neurological status while urgently escalating the change','Address immediate threats and reversible causes while obtaining medical help. Record the component findings and time of change.'],
   ['Wait for the next scheduled round to see whether attention returns','Scheduled observations do not replace an urgent response to new deterioration.'],
   ['Give an oral drink to test swallowing before assessing alertness','Reduced alertness can compromise airway protection. Assess safety before oral intake.'],
   ['Copy the earlier normal neurological entry because the patient was alert then','The record must reflect current findings. A previous normal examination does not describe the new state.']],
  teaching:['Which instruction is appropriate for a relative at the bedside?',
   ['Call staff immediately for increasing difficulty waking the patient or a new neurological change','Explain that changes from baseline matter. The relative should seek help rather than independently testing swallowing or walking.'],
   ['Let the patient sleep through all checks regardless of how difficult waking becomes','Rest is useful when stable, but reduced responsiveness requires reassessment.'],
   ['Give food whenever the patient briefly opens the eyes','Brief eye opening does not demonstrate safe swallowing or sustained alertness.'],
   ['Decide whether the change is serious by the temperature alone','Fever is one observation. Neurological deterioration can occur with a normal temperature.']],
  safety:['Which proposed action should the nurse correct?',
   ['Leave the newly drowsy patient unobserved until the routine neurological round','The acute change needs assessment and escalation now, with monitoring proportionate to the findings.'],
   ['Check oxygen saturation and blood glucose promptly','Hypoxia and hypoglycemia can contribute to altered consciousness and need rapid identification.'],
   ['Report the change and the last known baseline','A precise chronology helps the team determine urgency and possible causes.'],
   ['Document eye, verbal, and motor responses separately','Component observations communicate the neurological pattern better than an unexplained total alone.',gcs]],
  planning:['Which immediate outcome is most appropriate?',
   ['The cause of deterioration is urgently evaluated while airway and physiological safety are maintained','This directs care toward stabilization and timely assessment without promising a specific diagnosis or recovery.'],
   ['Complete routine paperwork before contacting the clinical team','Documentation is important but should not delay emergency assessment.'],
   ['Restore full alertness by repeated stimulation alone','Stimulation assesses responses; it does not treat all causes of reduced consciousness.'],
   ['Wait for a full diagnostic label before starting basic assessment','Airway and physiological assessment begin immediately while diagnostic evaluation proceeds.']],
  evaluation:['After the initial response, which finding still requires urgent escalation?',
   ['The patient becomes more difficult to rouse and the motor response deteriorates','Worsening responsiveness represents continued deterioration even if initial actions were completed. Reassess and escalate.'],
   ['The patient’s observations and change from baseline have been communicated clearly','Communication supports care, but it is a process measure rather than a sign of worsening.'],
   ['A reversible cause has been treated and alertness returns to the documented baseline','That is encouraging clinical improvement. Continue the indicated monitoring and review the cause.'],
   ['The receiving clinician is examining the patient at the bedside','Assessment is an appropriate response, although ongoing findings still determine clinical stability.']],
  reasoning:['Why should the nurse compare the examination with the documented baseline?',
   ['A new change in responsiveness may be clinically important even without a complete diagnostic score','Trend and context help identify deterioration early. Assessment tools support clinical judgment rather than replacing it.'],
   ['A previously normal score prevents later neurological deterioration','Baseline documentation permits comparison; it does not confer protection from a new event.'],
   ['One total score identifies the cause of every altered mental state','The GCS describes responses. It does not distinguish every neurological, drug-related, or metabolic cause.'],
   ['Only complete unresponsiveness warrants reassessment','Partial decline can be an early warning. Escalate clinically important changes before coma develops.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-neuroanatomy-assessment-basics-02':{
  source:headInjury,scenario:'Following a head injury, a patient develops unequal pupils. Earlier assessments recorded equal pupils, and no known eye treatment or pre-existing asymmetry explains the change.',
  recognition:['What is the priority interpretation?',
   ['A new focal neurological sign requiring urgent reassessment','New pupil inequality after head injury may indicate deterioration. Assess the full clinical picture and escalate promptly.'],
   ['A normal finding because some healthy people have unequal pupils','Physiological anisocoria can exist, but newly documented asymmetry after trauma requires assessment.'],
   ['Definite brain herniation established by pupil size alone','Herniation is a serious concern in the appropriate pattern. Pupil inequality alone does not prove a specific mechanism.'],
   ['A reason to stop the neurological observations','The change increases the need for observations and clinical review.']],
  action:['What should the nurse do now?',
   ['Reassess pupils and the overall neurological and physiological status while urgently notifying the clinician','Confirm the finding promptly if feasible without delaying escalation. Report the change from baseline and associated findings.'],
   ['Observe the pupils again at the next shift handoff','A delayed reassessment is inappropriate for a new post-traumatic focal sign.'],
   ['Administer dilating drops to make the pupils equal','Unprescribed drops do not treat the cause and can obscure further assessment.'],
   ['Record the earlier pupil sizes because they were checked by senior staff','Current objective findings must be documented even when they differ from an earlier assessment.']],
  teaching:['Which explanation is appropriate for the patient’s relative?',
   ['This new difference needs prompt assessment because it can indicate a change after the injury','Explain the need for review without declaring a specific diagnosis before assessment.'],
   ['Unequal pupils always prove irreversible brain damage','The finding has several possible causes; prognosis cannot be determined from pupil size alone.'],
   ['The change is safe if the patient can still speak','Preserved speech does not exclude neurological deterioration.'],
   ['Staff will only act if both pupils become fixed','Waiting for a more severe sign can delay necessary evaluation.']],
  safety:['Which proposal requires correction?',
   ['Treat the new pupil inequality as normal without checking the baseline or notifying the team','The documented change after head injury warrants urgent review, even if the patient is not yet deeply unconscious.'],
   ['Check pupil size, reaction, and associated limb or facial asymmetry','These component findings clarify the change and support clinical assessment.'],
   ['Review any eye medicine or pre-existing ocular finding without delaying urgent care','These factors may help explain anisocoria, but their review must not postpone necessary escalation.'],
   ['Prepare for the clinician’s decision about urgent imaging','New or evolving signs may prompt immediate CT and further management.']],
  planning:['Which immediate goal best fits the new finding?',
   ['Secure urgent neurological review with repeated documented observations','The goal is timely evaluation and detection of further deterioration while the cause is investigated.'],
   ['Make the pupil sizes identical before calling for help','Correcting the appearance is not the nursing target; the underlying cause needs assessment.'],
   ['Finish a routine eye-refraction test before escalation','Refraction does not address an acute post-traumatic neurological change.'],
   ['Confirm a permanent deficit before arranging further assessment','Timely intervention should occur before permanence is known.']],
  evaluation:['Which subsequent finding indicates further deterioration?',
   ['Pupil asymmetry persists and the patient develops new unilateral weakness','A new motor deficit strengthens concern for evolving intracranial pathology. Escalate immediately.'],
   ['A second competent assessor promptly confirms the recorded pupil sizes','Confirmation supports reliable assessment; it does not itself show clinical worsening.'],
   ['The clinician reviews the patient and orders the indicated investigation','This is appropriate escalation, not a neurological outcome.'],
   ['The patient remains physiologically stable while awaiting the urgent assessment','Stability is useful but does not explain or remove the need to investigate the focal sign.']],
  reasoning:['Why is an earlier record of equal pupils clinically relevant?',
   ['It indicates that the asymmetry is a change rather than an established baseline finding','A new focal sign after injury alters the assessment priority and should be communicated clearly.'],
   ['It excludes medication effects without a medicine review','Medicines can affect pupils. The timeline and exposure still need assessment.'],
   ['It makes further pupil observations unnecessary','Trend monitoring is more important after a new change.'],
   ['It confirms the exact intracranial location without imaging or examination','Pupil findings contribute to localization but do not independently establish the full diagnosis.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-neuroanatomy-assessment-basics-03':{
  source:'https://www.nmc.org.uk/standards/code/read-the-code-online/',scenario:'A patient reports new left-arm weakness. The current neurological note says only “strength normal” without testing and comparing the two sides.',
  recognition:['What is the principal problem with this record?',
   ['It does not objectively assess or describe the reported new asymmetry','A side-to-side motor examination and accurate current documentation are needed. A general label can conceal a focal change.'],
   ['The word normal is sufficient whenever the patient can move a finger','Small movement does not demonstrate normal power or symmetry. Assess the relevant movements and response.'],
   ['The patient’s report confirms a particular lesion without examination','The symptom is important but needs urgent objective assessment and appropriate clinical evaluation.'],
   ['A neurological note must contain imaging results before it can describe strength','Nurses document observed findings while medical investigation proceeds. Imaging is not required to record the examination.']],
  action:['What should the nurse prioritize?',
   ['Assess and compare limb power and associated neurological findings, then urgently escalate the new deficit','Record side-specific findings and onset. A new focal deficit can require a time-critical pathway.','https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng128/chapter/recommendations'],
   ['Copy yesterday’s strength findings until a doctor documents a diagnosis','Current assessment cannot be replaced by copied observations.'],
   ['Ask the patient to stop using the stronger arm to make the examination equal','Equality must not be manufactured. Compare actual ability using a consistent method.'],
   ['Delay assessment until the weakness prevents all movement','Partial weakness can represent an important early deficit.']],
  teaching:['Which explanation best supports the patient’s participation?',
   ['Tell us immediately when weakness starts or changes, even if you can still move the limb','The onset and progression help guide urgent assessment. Do not wait for complete paralysis.'],
   ['Report weakness only after it lasts several days','A new focal neurological change may need immediate evaluation.'],
   ['Movement of one finger proves the entire arm has normal strength','Power, drift, coordination, sensation, and function may need assessment; one movement is insufficient.'],
   ['The older note matters more than how the arm functions today','Prior findings provide comparison. Current symptoms and examination determine the present response.']],
  safety:['Which charting practice should the nurse correct?',
   ['Record both arms as normal without examining the reported weaker side','This creates an inaccurate record and can delay recognition of a focal deficit.'],
   ['Document the tested movement and side-specific result','Specific findings communicate the examination and permit meaningful comparison.'],
   ['Record the symptom onset and notify the appropriate clinical team','The timeline is important for acute neurological pathways.'],
   ['Assess for additional facial, speech, or leg changes','Associated findings may help identify an acute focal neurological syndrome.']],
  planning:['Which goal is appropriate for this immediate assessment?',
   ['Obtain an accurate current motor baseline and timely review of the new weakness','Objective comparison and escalation address both the documentation gap and the clinical risk.'],
   ['Use identical wording for every patient’s motor examination','Consistent methods are useful; identical findings regardless of examination are inaccurate.'],
   ['Prove recovery before documenting the initial deficit','Document the actual sequence and response, including deficits that later improve.'],
   ['Complete all routine care before considering the neurological complaint','The new deficit may have time-critical implications and needs prioritization.']],
  evaluation:['Which entry best demonstrates an improved assessment process?',
   ['The note records bilateral tested movements, the observed left-sided deficit, onset, and escalation','Specific current findings support safe communication and reassessment. Use the approved strength scale where applicable.'],
   ['The word normal is changed to fine without an examination','Changing a vague adjective does not provide objective assessment.'],
   ['The patient is reassured because an older record showed equal strength','The old record does not resolve a new symptom.'],
   ['Only the stronger side is examined to avoid tiring the patient','Adapt the examination when necessary, but record limitations and assess the affected side appropriately.']],
  reasoning:['Why does comparing both sides matter?',
   ['It helps detect focal asymmetry that an overall impression can miss','Side-specific observations provide a clearer trend and support urgent evaluation of new deficits.'],
   ['Symmetry alone proves every neurological function is normal','Some disease is bilateral or affects functions other than strength. Use the complete indicated examination.'],
   ['A strength score determines the cause without considering onset','The score describes performance. Cause and urgency also depend on the history and associated findings.'],
   ['An established normal baseline makes new weakness implausible','An acute event can occur after a normal examination. A change must be investigated.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-neuroanatomy-assessment-basics-04':{
  source:gcs,scenario:'Before a mental-status examination, the nurse learns that the patient cannot hear ordinary conversation without a hearing aid. The patient is awake and the usual aid is available.',
  recognition:['What must the nurse consider before interpreting answers?',
   ['A hearing barrier may interfere with the apparent verbal or command response','Make instructions accessible and document interfering factors. An unheard instruction is not evidence of cognitive failure.'],
   ['Hearing impairment automatically establishes a low cognitive score','Sensory access and cognition are different domains. Adjust the method before interpreting performance.'],
   ['A hearing aid makes every mental-status result invalid','Appropriate support can improve assessment validity; document its use.'],
   ['All hearing impairment reflects reduced consciousness','A patient can be fully alert while unable to hear ordinary speech.']],
  action:['What should the nurse do before starting the examination?',
   ['Ensure the aid functions, reduce noise, and confirm that the patient can hear or otherwise access instructions','Use the patient’s preferred effective method and document relevant limitations. Then assess the intended neurological function.'],
   ['Remove the aid to ensure every patient is tested identically','Identical delivery is not equitable when it prevents access to the task.'],
   ['Assign the lowest verbal score before trying accessible communication','An interfering factor should be documented rather than converted automatically into neurological failure.'],
   ['Ask a relative to supply every response instead of the patient','Collateral history is useful, but it does not replace the patient’s direct performance when accessible testing is possible.']],
  teaching:['Which explanation is most appropriate?',
   ['We will make sure you can access the questions so the assessment measures your responses fairly','Explain the process and ask the patient to signal when an instruction is unclear.'],
   ['Using your aid will unfairly improve your intelligence score','An aid removes a communication barrier; it is not an improper cognitive advantage.'],
   ['You must guess any instruction you cannot hear','Guessing reduces assessment validity. Ask for clarification or an accessible format.'],
   ['Once hearing loss is known, no neurological assessment is possible','Many observations and supported tasks remain possible. Record any component that genuinely cannot be tested.']],
  safety:['Which proposed assessment approach should the nurse correct?',
   ['Interpret failure to follow an inaudible command as definite cognitive impairment','The barrier must be addressed before drawing that conclusion. Document any remaining limitation.'],
   ['Keep the face visible and check the preferred method of communication','Visual and other accessible cues can improve understanding of the instructions.',hearing],
   ['Document the hearing support used during the examination','This context improves interpretation and comparison between assessments.'],
   ['Use an appropriate alternative when a component cannot be tested reliably','Acknowledge the limitation rather than inventing a response or an unjustified total.']],
  planning:['Which goal best supports valid assessment?',
   ['Obtain and document interpretable responses using effective communication support','Describe the actual findings and factors affecting testing. Support increases access without changing the neurological task being assessed.'],
   ['Complete every component regardless of whether instructions are accessible','A completed form can still contain invalid results. Record limitations when needed.'],
   ['Achieve the same score as the previous patient','The goal is accurate individual assessment, not a predetermined score.'],
   ['Exclude the patient from all decisions because hearing support is needed','Hearing loss alone does not establish incapacity or justify exclusion.']],
  evaluation:['Which finding best supports that the access barrier has been addressed?',
   ['With the functioning aid, the patient accurately repeats the instruction before performing it','This confirms access to that instruction. The actual task performance can then be interpreted in context.'],
   ['The aid is switched on but the patient still cannot identify the spoken instruction','Power alone does not establish effective hearing access. Adjust the method.'],
   ['A relative says the patient usually understands everything','Collateral information helps but does not establish access during the current test.'],
   ['The nurse finishes quickly without checking whether questions were heard','Speed does not demonstrate assessment validity.']],
  reasoning:['Why should an interfering hearing problem be documented?',
   ['It can alter the observed response without representing reduced consciousness or cognition','A clear record distinguishes neurological findings from testing limitations and supports meaningful reassessment.'],
   ['It allows all abnormal responses to be ignored permanently','After optimizing access, persistent abnormalities still require appropriate assessment.'],
   ['It proves that every change is caused by the hearing aid','The aid is one factor. New clinical changes still require evaluation.'],
   ['It replaces the need to record the actual examination findings','Context supplements objective findings; it does not substitute for them.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-metabolic-bone-disorders-01':{
  source:bone,scenario:'An older adult with osteoporosis has loose rugs, poor nighttime lighting, and no stable support for stepping into the bath. The patient wishes to continue living at home.',
  recognition:['Which concern should guide the home-safety plan?',
   ['Modifiable fall hazards increase an already elevated fracture risk','Osteoporosis reduces bone strength. Reducing falls complements treatment of the bone disease.'],
   ['A painless skeleton means the osteoporosis is inactive','Osteoporosis may be silent until fracture. Pain absence does not establish normal strength.'],
   ['Every fall hazard can be removed by a calcium supplement alone','Adequate calcium supports bone health but cannot remove environmental hazards.'],
   ['Living at home is impossible for every person with osteoporosis','Feasible modifications and support can help preserve safe independence.']],
  action:['Which nursing recommendation best addresses the stated hazards?',
   ['Remove loose rugs, improve lighting, and arrange suitable bath supports after assessing the home','Target the observed trip and transfer risks with practical changes and the patient’s involvement.'],
   ['Recommend indefinite bed rest to prevent any fall','Prolonged inactivity weakens function and can worsen bone and fall risk. Use an individualized activity plan.'],
   ['Keep the rugs but advise the patient to remember their positions','Awareness alone is less reliable than removing a clear trip hazard.'],
   ['Focus only on the next DXA date and defer home changes','Bone-density follow-up matters, but avoidable fall hazards can be addressed now.']],
  teaching:['Which statement shows appropriate understanding?',
   ['I will improve the walking route and ask about balance and strengthening activities suited to me','Home modification and appropriate exercise address fall risk while supporting function.'],
   ['I do not need treatment because my bones do not hurt','A lack of pain does not exclude osteoporosis or fracture risk.'],
   ['I should stop all weight-bearing activity permanently','Safe weight-bearing and strengthening activity can benefit bone and function. Tailor it to fracture risk.'],
   ['A grab bar can be replaced by an unsecured towel rail','Supports must be designed and installed to bear weight safely.']],
  safety:['Which proposal should the nurse challenge?',
   ['Use an unstable chair as a step into the bath','An unstable support can cause a fall. Arrange suitable equipment and assistance based on the transfer assessment.'],
   ['Keep a clear, well-lit route to the bathroom','This reduces night-time trip hazards.'],
   ['Review medicines and symptoms that could contribute to falls','Sedation, dizziness, or orthostatic symptoms can compound environmental risks.'],
   ['Discuss appropriate footwear and mobility support','Suitable footwear and properly fitted aids can improve stability when indicated.']],
  planning:['Which goal best fits this patient’s preference and risk?',
   ['Maintain safe mobility at home through agreed hazard reduction and individualized bone-health care','The plan combines independence, fall prevention, and management of osteoporosis rather than relying on one measure.'],
   ['Eliminate all movement to guarantee that no fracture occurs','Immobility causes harm and cannot guarantee prevention of every fracture.'],
   ['Judge safety only by whether the home looks tidy','Specific walking, lighting, transfer, and support risks matter more than appearance alone.'],
   ['Complete supplement teaching without discussing the actual bath transfer','Nutrition advice does not resolve the observed transfer hazard.']],
  evaluation:['Which finding best demonstrates progress on the home-safety goal?',
   ['The patient demonstrates a safe bath transfer using stable fitted support and the main trip hazards are removed','Observed safe function and environmental changes provide direct evidence of progress.'],
   ['The patient has memorized a fall-prevention leaflet but the rugs and unstable support remain','Knowledge alone has not changed the identified hazards.'],
   ['The patient reports no bone pain and therefore resumes using the unstable chair','Pain is not a measure of environmental safety or bone strength.'],
   ['The patient stays in bed all day and has no immediate fall','Avoiding movement does not demonstrate a sustainable plan for safe independence.']],
  reasoning:['Why does fall prevention matter even when osteoporosis medication is prescribed?',
   ['Medication and environmental measures address different contributors to fracture risk','Bone treatment can reduce fragility, while hazard reduction reduces the chance of a fall. Both remain relevant.'],
   ['A first dose immediately restores normal strength to every bone','Bone treatments act over time and do not eliminate all fracture risk.'],
   ['Every fracture in osteoporosis follows a major accident','Fragile bones may fracture with relatively minor trauma or stress.'],
   ['Home modifications replace the need for clinical osteoporosis review','Fall prevention complements, rather than completes, assessment and treatment of the bone disease.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-metabolic-bone-disorders-02':{
  source:alendronate,scenario:'A patient prescribed a standard immediate-release oral alendronate tablet takes it at bedtime with a sip of water and lies down immediately. The patient has no current swallowing symptoms.',
  recognition:['Which risk is most directly associated with this administration pattern?',
   ['Esophageal irritation or injury from incorrect tablet administration','Alendronate requires the specified water intake and upright interval to reduce esophageal exposure. Check the exact prescription and formulation.'],
   ['Improved absorption because the patient lies down','Recumbency does not improve administration safety or establish better absorption.'],
   ['An inevitable fracture caused by the bedtime dose','Incorrect timing creates a medication-safety concern, but it does not prove an immediate fracture.'],
   ['A normal administration method for every oral osteoporosis medicine','Different formulations have different instructions. Standard alendronate tablets have specific fasting and posture requirements.']],
  action:['What should the nurse do?',
   ['Correct the technique to the prescribed morning fasting dose with a full glass of plain water and the required upright interval','For this formulation, remain upright for at least 30 minutes and until after the first food of the day. Confirm suitability if the patient cannot follow the instructions.'],
   ['Recommend taking the tablet in bed with milk to protect the stomach','Milk can interfere with absorption, and recumbency increases esophageal risk.'],
   ['Crush the tablet into the evening meal without checking the product','This does not follow the standard tablet directions. Clarify an alternative regimen with the prescriber if swallowing or posture is a problem.'],
   ['Double the next dose to compensate for poor technique','Do not independently double the dose. Review missed-dose and administration instructions for the prescribed schedule.']],
  teaching:['Which statement shows correct understanding for this tablet?',
   ['I will take it on arising with plain water, wait at least 30 minutes before other intake, and remain upright as directed','These instructions address absorption and esophageal safety. Follow the exact product and dosing schedule.'],
   ['I will take calcium at the same moment to improve alendronate absorption','Calcium and other oral products can interfere; separate them according to the product instructions.'],
   ['I can lie down as soon as I swallow the tablet if I have no pain','Symptoms need not occur immediately. The upright interval is preventive.'],
   ['If swallowing becomes painful, I should continue until the next annual visit','New dysphagia, painful swallowing, or retrosternal pain requires prompt advice and the label’s instructions about stopping further doses pending review.']],
  safety:['Which proposed instruction needs correction?',
   ['Continue the bedtime dose and lie flat immediately afterward','This exposes the esophagus to avoidable risk and does not follow standard alendronate administration guidance.'],
   ['Check whether the patient can remain upright for the required interval','Inability to comply can make the formulation unsuitable and requires prescriber review.'],
   ['Review other oral medicines and supplements for timing conflicts','Food, drinks other than water, and some oral products can impair absorption.'],
   ['Teach the patient to report new swallowing or chest symptoms','These can indicate esophageal irritation and require prompt review.']],
  planning:['Which goal best fits this medication review?',
   ['The patient demonstrates a feasible, product-correct dosing routine without esophageal warning symptoms','A safe routine must account for fasting, water, posture, and the patient’s abilities. Arrange an alternative if necessary.'],
   ['The patient takes the tablet at any time as long as the weekly total is unchanged','Correct dose alone is insufficient when administration technique affects safety and absorption.'],
   ['Stop all osteoporosis management because this technique was wrong','The regimen needs correction or reassessment, not abandonment of fracture-risk management.'],
   ['Use a smaller water volume whenever swallowing feels difficult','Difficulty swallowing calls for assessment; it is not a reason to depart from safe administration instructions.']],
  evaluation:['Which finding best supports that the teaching was effective?',
   ['The patient accurately describes the morning dose, full water intake, fasting interval, and upright period','Teach-back of all key steps supports correct use. Reassess practical barriers and warning symptoms.'],
   ['The patient remembers the drug name but still takes it lying in bed','Drug-name recall does not demonstrate safe administration.'],
   ['The patient has no symptoms after one incorrectly taken dose','A symptom-free event does not validate a risky routine.'],
   ['The patient takes it with coffee because the dose is still on an empty stomach','Plain water is required for the standard tablet. Coffee is not an equivalent substitute.']],
  reasoning:['Why is the upright interval included in the instructions?',
   ['It helps reduce prolonged contact of the tablet with the esophagus','The water and posture requirements support delivery to the stomach and reduce esophageal injury risk.'],
   ['It makes the tablet bypass the gastrointestinal tract entirely','The medicine is still absorbed through the gastrointestinal tract.'],
   ['It immediately increases bone density after each dose','Posture protects administration safety; bone effects develop with treatment over time.'],
   ['It allows all food and calcium products to be taken simultaneously','The fasting and separation instructions still apply.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-metabolic-bone-disorders-03':{
  source:vitaminD,scenario:'An adult with limited nutritional intake has persistent bone discomfort and proximal muscle weakness. The clinician is investigating possible vitamin D deficiency and impaired bone mineralization.',
  recognition:['Which problem is most relevant to the assessment?',
   ['Possible vitamin D deficiency with impaired mineralization, requiring investigation','Vitamin D supports calcium absorption and bone mineralization. The symptoms raise concern but do not establish a diagnosis without assessment.'],
   ['Osteoporosis proven by bone pain alone','Osteoporosis is often silent and is assessed by fracture risk and appropriate testing. Pain alone does not diagnose it.'],
   ['A need for high-dose vitamin D without any review','Replacement depends on the clinical and laboratory assessment, comorbidities, and prescribed regimen.'],
   ['A purely behavioral cause established by poor intake','Limited intake warrants nutritional assessment rather than dismissal of physical symptoms.']],
  action:['What should the nurse prioritize within the evaluation plan?',
   ['Assess intake and functional safety, and coordinate the prescribed deficiency and bone-health workup','Identify nutritional barriers, mobility needs, and the ordered tests. Treatment is based on the confirmed problem and risk.'],
   ['Give an unprescribed megadose of vitamin D to test whether symptoms improve','Excessive vitamin D can cause toxicity. Therapeutic dosing needs clinical direction.'],
   ['Recommend strenuous exercise despite current weakness and bone pain','Exercise can support health when appropriate, but current symptoms require safety assessment and a tailored plan.'],
   ['Tell the patient that calcium intake alone makes vitamin D assessment unnecessary','Calcium and vitamin D have related but distinct roles; adequate calcium does not exclude vitamin D deficiency.']],
  teaching:['Which statement shows appropriate understanding?',
   ['I will follow the assessed nutrition and replacement plan rather than taking extra high doses on my own','Adequate intake and prescribed replacement address deficiency while avoiding unnecessary toxicity risk.'],
   ['Vitamin D is unrelated to how my body uses calcium','Vitamin D helps the body absorb and use calcium for bone health.'],
   ['Bone pain confirms that supplements will cure every possible cause','Pain has several causes. Investigation and response assessment remain necessary.'],
   ['Taking more than the prescribed dose always speeds recovery safely','Higher doses are not automatically better and can cause harm.']],
  safety:['Which proposal should the nurse challenge?',
   ['Use prolonged unmonitored high-dose vitamin D because the symptoms suggest deficiency','Suspected deficiency requires assessment and a safe prescribed regimen. Excessive dosing can cause hypercalcemia and other harm.'],
   ['Review all supplements the patient already takes','Multiple products can duplicate doses and affect the overall intake.'],
   ['Arrange assistance for transfers if proximal weakness limits safety','Functional impairment can increase fall risk while the cause is being treated.'],
   ['Evaluate the nutritional reasons for low intake','Food access, appetite, swallowing, and other barriers can affect the treatment plan.']],
  planning:['Which goal is appropriate during this workup?',
   ['Identify the cause of the symptoms and correct confirmed nutritional deficits while maintaining safe function','The goal links investigation, tailored treatment, and the patient’s immediate mobility needs.'],
   ['Normalize every symptom before obtaining any history or tests','Assessment guides therapy and should not be postponed until symptoms resolve.'],
   ['Use the same replacement dose for all adults regardless of findings','Dose and monitoring depend on the individual problem and clinical circumstances.'],
   ['Avoid all activity permanently to protect the bones','Activity should be adapted to the current risk, not prohibited indefinitely without indication.']],
  evaluation:['Which finding best supports benefit from the clinician-directed plan?',
   ['Relevant deficiency markers and function improve on scheduled reassessment','Use the prescribed laboratory and clinical outcomes, including strength, pain, intake, and mobility.'],
   ['The patient takes several overlapping supplements but has not been reassessed','Product use alone does not establish benefit or safety. Review total intake and outcomes.'],
   ['Pain persists but the label says bone health, so treatment is considered complete','A product claim does not substitute for reassessment of unresolved symptoms.'],
   ['The patient stops eating because a supplement supplies vitamin D','A supplement does not replace adequate energy, protein, and other nutrients.']],
  reasoning:['Why should nutrition and laboratory findings be assessed together?',
   ['Symptoms and low intake suggest risk, while clinical testing helps determine the actual deficit and treatment','Combining evidence avoids diagnosing or dosing from symptoms alone.'],
   ['A normal body weight excludes vitamin deficiency','A person can have micronutrient deficiency at different body weights.'],
   ['Every proximal weakness pattern is caused by vitamin D deficiency','Neurological, muscular, endocrine, and other causes may need consideration.'],
   ['A dietary history measures the serum vitamin D concentration directly','History identifies risk and barriers; laboratory testing measures the relevant biochemical marker.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-metabolic-bone-disorders-04':{
  source:bone,scenario:'A patient with known osteoporosis develops sudden severe back pain after lifting a light object. The current height is lower than an earlier documented measurement.',
  recognition:['Which complication should the nurse suspect?',
   ['A possible vertebral fragility fracture','Sudden back pain after minor stress with height loss can indicate a vertebral fracture. Clinical assessment and imaging establish the cause.'],
   ['A normal painless feature of osteoporosis','The new severe pain requires evaluation and should not be dismissed as expected aging.'],
   ['A confirmed lumbar disc prolapse from the pain alone','Disc disease is a possible differential, but the fragility and height-loss pattern raises particular fracture concern.'],
   ['A need to repeat the lifting movement to confirm the source','Provoking the movement may worsen pain or injury and is not a diagnostic substitute.']],
  action:['What should the nurse prioritize?',
   ['Arrange prompt clinical assessment, support comfortable safe movement, and check for neurological warning signs','Assess pain, function, trauma history, and bowel, bladder, or neurological changes. New deficits require emergency escalation.'],
   ['Encourage repeated loaded bending to loosen the spine','Exercise may later be prescribed, but forced loaded movement is inappropriate before assessing a possible acute fracture.'],
   ['Treat the pain as harmless because the object was light','Fragile vertebrae can fracture after relatively minor stress.'],
   ['Delay review until the next routine bone-density scan','DXA follow-up does not replace evaluation of a new painful event.']],
  teaching:['Which explanation should the nurse give?',
   ['A fragile vertebra can fracture with minor strain, so this new pain needs assessment','Explain the concern without declaring a confirmed fracture. Review symptoms requiring emergency care.'],
   ['A fracture is impossible without a major fall','Osteoporotic fractures can occur with low-energy stress.'],
   ['Height loss proves that no treatment can help','Height loss may signal vertebral changes, but assessment can guide pain care and further fracture prevention.'],
   ['You should test recovery by lifting a heavier object tomorrow','Return to activity should follow assessment and a safe rehabilitation plan.']],
  safety:['Which proposal should the nurse challenge?',
   ['Dismiss the severe new pain and advise unrestricted heavy lifting','The presentation needs prompt assessment for fracture and related complications before high-load activity.'],
   ['Ask about new leg weakness, saddle sensory change, and bladder symptoms','These are neurological warning findings that can change the urgency of spinal assessment.'],
   ['Provide prescribed analgesia while arranging assessment','Pain relief supports comfort but does not replace investigation.'],
   ['Review safe transfers and the need for assistance','Pain and possible fracture can impair mobility and increase falls.']],
  planning:['Which goal is appropriate for this episode?',
   ['Identify and manage the acute spinal problem while preserving safe mobility and reviewing future fracture risk','The plan addresses the new event and continuing osteoporosis care together.'],
   ['Keep the patient immobile indefinitely after the pain settles','Prolonged immobility has harms; recovery and rehabilitation should be individualized.'],
   ['Judge recovery only by the next DXA number','DXA measures bone density, not the immediate pain, neurological state, or functional recovery.'],
   ['Assume calcium alone will relieve an acute fracture immediately','Nutrition supports bone health but is not acute fracture assessment or analgesia.']],
  evaluation:['Which new finding requires emergency escalation during follow-up?',
   ['New leg weakness with difficulty passing urine','A new neurological and bladder change can indicate serious neural compression and needs emergency assessment.'],
   ['Pain improves with the prescribed plan and transfers become safer','This supports clinical progress, with continued follow-up as indicated.'],
   ['The patient accurately explains the agreed activity precautions','Understanding supports care but is not a sign of deterioration.'],
   ['The treating team has arranged appropriate imaging','This is a diagnostic step rather than an emergency clinical change.']],
  reasoning:['Why does a low-energy lifting event remain important in osteoporosis?',
   ['Reduced bone strength allows fracture after stresses that stronger bone might tolerate','The modest trigger does not justify dismissing new severe pain or height loss.'],
   ['Osteoporosis causes every episode of back pain by the same mechanism','Several causes remain possible. Assess the current presentation rather than assuming.'],
   ['A painless baseline excludes a later vertebral fracture','Osteoporosis often lacks symptoms until a fracture occurs.'],
   ['Height measurement alone determines the exact fracture level','Height loss is a clue; localization and diagnosis need clinical assessment and appropriate imaging.']],
 },
});

Object.assign(sensoryCases,{
 'SIT-NP4-documentation-professional-development-01':{
  source:recordStandard,scenario:'A nurse finds that today’s neurological entry was copied from yesterday despite a new deficit today. The current patient has not yet had the change assessed or escalated.',
  recognition:['What is the most important concern?',
   ['The copied entry conceals a current clinical change','Inaccurate carry-forward information can delay recognition and treatment. The patient and record both require attention.'],
   ['A formatting problem with no clinical consequence','The discrepancy concerns a new deficit, not merely style.'],
   ['A valid record because yesterday’s finding was accurate then','An accurate past observation does not describe today’s new condition.'],
   ['A reason to delete every prior neurological record','Earlier entries provide history and should remain traceable; correct the current error through the approved process.']],
  action:['What should the nurse prioritize?',
   ['Assess and escalate the new deficit, then correct the record with a traceable current entry','Patient care must not wait for paperwork. Document the actual findings, timing, and actions without falsifying the earlier history.'],
   ['Change the note silently and omit the patient assessment','A cosmetic correction does not address the clinical change and may destroy the audit trail.'],
   ['Wait for the original author to return before assessing the patient','The present nurse must respond to the current safety concern.'],
   ['Copy a different normal note to replace the first one','Another unverified entry would repeat the same error.']],
  teaching:['Which principle should guide feedback to the team?',
   ['Every carried-forward finding must be checked against the current assessment','Templates can support efficiency, but the author remains responsible for accuracy and current relevance.'],
   ['A copied note is safe whenever another nurse signed the original','A prior signature does not verify the present patient state.'],
   ['Only abnormal findings need an actual assessment','Normal findings must also be based on observations rather than assumptions.'],
   ['Correcting a record means erasing all evidence of the original entry','Use the approved amendment process and preserve traceability.']],
  safety:['Which proposed response should the nurse challenge?',
   ['Leave the copied normal findings unchanged and do not report the new deficit','This hides a potentially important clinical change and creates an inaccurate record.'],
   ['Document the current examination and the time the change was recognized','Specific current information supports clinical decision-making.'],
   ['Use the approved correction or addendum process','A traceable correction preserves record integrity.'],
   ['Escalate the patient’s new neurological change promptly','Clinical safety takes priority while the documentation problem is resolved.']],
  planning:['Which outcome best fits the corrective plan?',
   ['The patient receives timely assessment and the record accurately represents the current findings and actions','Both clinical response and record integrity must be addressed.'],
   ['The chart looks complete even if no one has examined the patient','Completeness of fields does not establish truth or safe care.'],
   ['The copied note is removed without any replacement or audit trail','This can obscure the sequence and fails to provide the current assessment.'],
   ['The team stops using every electronic template permanently','The problem is unverified content; system changes should target the actual risk.']],
  evaluation:['Which finding best demonstrates that the issue was resolved?',
   ['A current objective assessment and escalation are documented with a traceable correction of the erroneous entry','The record now supports care and shows how the error was addressed.'],
   ['The wording has changed but the patient has not been assessed','The main clinical risk remains.'],
   ['The nurse promises to be careful but the incorrect note remains active','An intention does not correct the current record or care gap.'],
   ['The new deficit is recorded only in an informal private message','Relevant clinical information must reach the care team and the authorized record.']],
  reasoning:['Why is unverified carry-forward documentation unsafe?',
   ['It can make old findings appear current and conceal deterioration','Care decisions depend on accurate time-linked observations. Verify rather than merely reproduce text.'],
   ['Electronic records cannot ever be corrected','Approved correction processes preserve both accuracy and traceability.'],
   ['A complete form proves that every assessment was performed','Entries require evidence from the actual assessment.'],
   ['The previous author is solely responsible for every future copied entry','Each author remains accountable for the accuracy of their own documentation.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-documentation-professional-development-04':{
  source:recordStandard,scenario:'A discharge-record audit finds that a nurse writes “teaching done” without recording what the patient could explain or demonstrate. The audit feedback asks for evidence of the actual response.',
  recognition:['What important information is missing?',
   ['The patient’s demonstrated understanding and remaining learning needs','A statement that teaching occurred does not show whether the patient can carry out the plan.'],
   ['Only the number of pages in the leaflet','Material length does not establish comprehension or practical ability.'],
   ['A guarantee that no complication will ever occur','Even effective teaching cannot guarantee every future outcome.'],
   ['The nurse’s opinion that the patient seems pleasant','A general impression is not evidence of self-care understanding.']],
  action:['Which documentation improvement is most useful?',
   ['Record specific teach-back or demonstration findings and any follow-up needed','Describe the task, observed response, correction, and unresolved barrier rather than a vague completion phrase.'],
   ['Replace teaching done with education complete without assessing the response','A different label does not add evidence.'],
   ['Record that the patient understands whenever a leaflet is handed over','Delivery of written material does not establish understanding.'],
   ['Invent a successful demonstration to satisfy the audit form','Documentation must reflect what actually occurred.']],
  teaching:['Which principle should guide the nurse’s revised practice?',
   ['Documentation should show how the patient responded to the essential instructions','Record observed evidence and remaining needs using respectful, objective language.'],
   ['A signature is always enough to prove safe self-care','A signature may document a process step but not the ability to perform the regimen.'],
   ['Every patient response should be copied from a standard example','The record must describe the individual response.'],
   ['Unresolved questions should be omitted to keep discharge notes brief','Relevant gaps and the plan to address them are important safety information.']],
  safety:['Which proposed note should the nurse correct?',
   ['Patient fully understands, although the patient could not explain the medicine schedule','The conclusion conflicts with the observed response. Record the gap and the additional support or teaching arranged.'],
   ['Patient demonstrated the inhaler steps correctly after coaching','An observed task and response provide useful evidence.'],
   ['Patient remains unsure of one dose; pharmacist review arranged','The entry identifies a risk and the action taken.'],
   ['Written instructions provided in the patient’s preferred accessible format','This is a useful process detail when paired with the actual response.']],
  planning:['Which goal best fits the audit feedback?',
   ['Discharge entries consistently describe individual responses and actions on remaining gaps','The goal is useful accurate communication, not merely longer notes.'],
   ['Increase the note length without changing the evidence recorded','Length alone does not improve clinical value.'],
   ['Use identical successful outcomes for every patient','Predetermined outcomes can misrepresent actual understanding.'],
   ['Avoid asking patients questions so no learning gaps appear','Assessment is necessary to identify and address risk.']],
  evaluation:['Which entry best demonstrates the intended improvement?',
   ['Patient used the schedule to identify each dose correctly; unclear wound-care step was retaught and demonstrated','The note records specific understanding, a gap, and the response to additional teaching.'],
   ['All education completed as usual','The same vague phrase remains.'],
   ['Leaflet contains six pages and was printed clearly','Material quality matters, but the patient response is still missing.'],
   ['Patient signed the form; understanding was not checked','The form does not establish the targeted outcome.']],
  reasoning:['Why is the patient response more informative than teaching done alone?',
   ['It shows whether information was usable and what support is still needed','The next clinician can see actual evidence rather than infer success from delivery.'],
   ['It eliminates the need for any future follow-up','Understanding can change and complications may still arise.'],
   ['It proves the nurse caused every later outcome','Documentation supports continuity but does not establish sole causation.'],
   ['It requires recording a successful result even when teaching failed','Accuracy includes unresolved needs and the plan to address them.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-documentation-professional-development-02-R2':{
  source:recordStandard,scenario:'After a verified near miss, a nurse writes a learning plan that says only “be more careful.” The plan identifies no skill gap, practice activity, or way to reassess competence.',
  recognition:['What is the main weakness of this plan?',
   ['It lacks specific actions and evidence for improvement','A useful learning plan connects the identified problem to practice, feedback, and reassessment.'],
   ['It acknowledges a learning need, which is always inappropriate','Honest reflection and constructive feedback support professional development.'],
   ['It should guarantee that no error will ever occur again','A guarantee is unrealistic; measurable risk reduction and demonstrated competence are appropriate.'],
   ['It must focus on blame rather than the process involved','Learning should address actual contributing factors and safe practice.']],
  action:['Which revision is most useful?',
   ['Identify the relevant gap, arrange supervised practice and feedback, and set a reassessment date','The plan should produce observable evidence of safer performance. Also address system contributors through the appropriate process.'],
   ['Repeat be careful in several places without defining the behavior','Repetition does not make the action measurable.'],
   ['Attend any unrelated seminar and assume the gap is closed','Learning activity should address the identified need.'],
   ['Hide the near miss to avoid future feedback','Concealment prevents learning and may leave the risk unaddressed.']],
  teaching:['Which statement reflects appropriate professional learning?',
   ['I will use feedback to target the specific skill and demonstrate improvement','Reflection should lead to action and review rather than a vague intention alone.'],
   ['A certificate automatically proves competence in every clinical skill','Attendance is not equivalent to observed practical competence.'],
   ['Once I identify a gap, I should never perform any care again','Seek appropriate supervision and development within competence; a targeted gap does not invalidate all practice.'],
   ['A near miss is irrelevant because no harm occurred','Near misses can reveal hazards before harm occurs.']],
  safety:['Which proposal should the nurse challenge?',
   ['Close the learning issue solely because be more careful has been written','The plan needs a relevant action and a way to check improvement.'],
   ['Arrange supervision for the identified skill until competence is demonstrated','Supervision protects patients while learning occurs.'],
   ['Review contributing workflow and communication factors','Individual learning and system improvement can both be needed.'],
   ['Document feedback and the agreed reassessment method','A clear plan supports accountability and follow-through.']],
  planning:['Which goal best fits the improvement plan?',
   ['Demonstrate the targeted safe skill consistently by the agreed review date','The outcome is observable and linked to the actual gap.'],
   ['Accumulate the largest possible number of unrelated certificates','Quantity of activity does not establish relevance or competence.'],
   ['Avoid every discussion of the near miss','Constructive discussion is needed to understand and reduce the risk.'],
   ['Promise perfection without any supervised practice','A promise does not provide evidence of improvement.']],
  evaluation:['Which finding best supports closure of the targeted learning gap?',
   ['Observed practice meets the agreed criteria and feedback confirms the relevant steps','Use actual performance and continued monitoring rather than attendance alone.'],
   ['The nurse has rewritten the plan in a more attractive format','Presentation does not demonstrate competence.'],
   ['The review date has passed without any assessment','Time elapsed is not evidence of improvement.'],
   ['The nurse attended a session but cannot perform the relevant task','Additional practice and support are still needed.']],
  reasoning:['Why should the plan include reassessment?',
   ['It tests whether learning has changed the relevant clinical performance','A learning activity is a means; safer demonstrated practice is the intended result.'],
   ['It guarantees that all future errors are impossible','Reassessment reduces uncertainty but cannot guarantee perfection.'],
   ['It replaces the need to address system hazards','Skill development and system changes may both be necessary.'],
   ['It is only useful when a patient was harmed','Near misses can justify learning and risk reduction before harm occurs.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-documentation-professional-development-03-R2':{
  source:dataPrivacy,scenario:'In a Philippine health facility, a patient asks how to request a copy of their own clinical information. A staff member says confidentiality means patients can never request access to their records.',
  recognition:['What is wrong with the staff member’s statement?',
   ['Confidentiality does not create a blanket prohibition on the data subject’s access','The Data Privacy Act recognizes reasonable access rights. Requests should follow the lawful identity, scope, and disclosure process.'],
   ['Every record must be published online whenever a patient asks','Access rights do not authorize public disclosure or exposure of other people’s information.'],
   ['Only relatives have any right to request patient information','The patient is the data subject; relatives require appropriate authority or another lawful basis.'],
   ['The bedside nurse must release every file immediately without verification','Requests require the authorized process and relevant safeguards.']],
  action:['What should the nurse do?',
   ['Explain the facility’s authorized records-request process and assist the patient to use it','Verify identity and route the request appropriately. Seek privacy or records-officer advice for questions about scope or lawful limitations.'],
   ['Refuse the request solely by repeating confidentiality','A blanket refusal ignores the patient’s reasonable access rights.'],
   ['Send the entire file to an unverified social-media account','An insecure or unverified disclosure can breach privacy.'],
   ['Give the patient another person’s record as an example','Third-party information must not be disclosed without a lawful basis.']],
  teaching:['Which explanation is accurate?',
   ['You can request access to your personal information through the facility’s verified process','Explain practical steps and contact points without promising disclosure beyond the law or the request’s scope.'],
   ['Confidentiality means you can never ask what information is held about you','The law provides data-subject information and access rights.'],
   ['A friend may collect all records without any identity or authority check','Identity and authority safeguards remain necessary.'],
   ['Requesting a copy allows staff to delete the original clinical record','Access is different from retention and correction duties. The official record must be managed lawfully.']],
  safety:['Which proposed response should the nurse challenge?',
   ['Deny all patient record requests on the assumption that privacy forbids self-access','Apply the actual access provisions and authorized process rather than a false absolute rule.'],
   ['Confirm the requester’s identity before disclosure','Verification helps ensure information reaches the authorized person.'],
   ['Refer uncertainty about third-party information to the privacy or records officer','A qualified review can address scope and lawful safeguards.'],
   ['Provide the patient with the correct request contact and steps','Practical assistance supports lawful access.']],
  planning:['Which outcome best fits the patient’s request?',
   ['The patient can submit a verified access request and receive a lawful response through the proper channel','The plan supports the right while protecting identity and other sensitive information.'],
   ['The entire record is released publicly to avoid any delay','Public disclosure is neither necessary nor an appropriate access method.'],
   ['The request is ignored because no treatment is involved','Data-access rights are not limited to an immediate treatment decision.'],
   ['Every relative receives a copy regardless of patient authority','Disclosure to relatives requires an appropriate legal basis.']],
  evaluation:['Which finding best shows that the nursing assistance was effective?',
   ['The patient correctly identifies the request channel and submits the required verified request','This demonstrates access to the process. The authorized office determines the lawful response.'],
   ['The patient stops asking after being told the request is forbidden','Silence after misinformation does not establish a successful outcome.'],
   ['A file is emailed to an unverified address without checking the recipient','This creates a privacy risk rather than demonstrating safe access.'],
   ['The nurse promises every document immediately without consulting the process','An unsupported promise can misstate the scope and requirements.']],
  reasoning:['Why can confidentiality and patient access coexist?',
   ['Privacy protects against unauthorized disclosure while recognizing the data subject’s lawful rights','The correct response is verified authorized access, not either unrestricted disclosure or blanket denial.'],
   ['The patient owns every other person’s information in the same system','Access rights concern the data subject’s information and remain subject to lawful safeguards.'],
   ['A record request cancels the need to keep information secure','Security obligations continue during access and transfer.'],
   ['Only staff convenience determines whether access is allowed','Applicable law and authorized procedures govern the request.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-malnutrition-01':{
  source:oralNutrition,scenario:'An older patient has loose dentures, painful chewing, declining food intake, and documented unintentional weight loss. The patient wants to eat but avoids foods that hurt.',
  recognition:['Which contributor to poor intake should the nurse address?',
   ['An oral and denture problem is creating a practical eating barrier','Pain and poor denture fit can limit food choice and intake. Assess oral health and nutritional risk together.'],
   ['A voluntary refusal of nutrition established by leaving food','The patient wants to eat and reports pain. Identify the barrier rather than labeling refusal.'],
   ['Normal aging that does not need assessment','Weight loss and painful chewing require evaluation.'],
   ['A problem solved solely by recording the current weight','Weight documents impact but does not correct the cause of reduced intake.']],
  action:['What should the nurse prioritize?',
   ['Assess the mouth and nutrition, arrange dental review, and provide a suitable interim eating plan','Choose tolerable nutritious foods with the patient while the cause is addressed. Assess swallowing separately if concerns are present.'],
   ['Force hard foods to strengthen the gums despite pain','This can worsen discomfort and reduce intake further.'],
   ['Remove all food until the dentures are repaired','Interim nutrition and hydration support are needed rather than prolonged fasting.'],
   ['Use denture adhesive as the only response without assessing the fit or pain','Selected products may help, but painful loose dentures require proper review.']],
  teaching:['Which explanation best supports the patient?',
   ['We can address the dental problem and choose tolerable foods that still meet your nutritional needs','Adaptation should preserve energy and nutrient intake while arranging definitive oral care.'],
   ['Painful eating is unavoidable, so weight loss should be accepted','The barrier deserves treatment and nutritional support.'],
   ['Soft food always means a nutritionally complete diet','Texture and nutrient adequacy are separate considerations; assess both.'],
   ['A normal-looking denture never needs professional adjustment','Fit, pain, and oral findings matter even when a device appears intact.']],
  safety:['Which proposed plan should the nurse challenge?',
   ['Document poor cooperation and leave the painful eating barrier unaddressed','This misinterprets the stated problem and can prolong inadequate intake.'],
   ['Monitor intake and weight trends using an appropriate nutrition screen','Screening identifies risk and guides support.',nutrition],
   ['Arrange oral assessment and denture review','Addressing the cause can improve comfort and intake.'],
   ['Offer suitable nutrient-dense foods within the assessed swallowing ability','A practical interim plan can reduce further nutritional decline.']],
  planning:['Which goal best fits this situation?',
   ['Improve comfortable adequate intake while resolving the oral barrier and monitoring nutritional recovery','The goal combines cause, intake, and outcome rather than treating weight alone.'],
   ['Make the patient finish the standard tray regardless of pain','Coercion does not address the barrier or patient preference.'],
   ['Delay every nutrition measure until the dental appointment','Support is needed while waiting.'],
   ['Assume all weight loss is due to dentures without broader assessment','The obvious barrier matters, but other contributors may coexist.']],
  evaluation:['Which finding best supports benefit from the coordinated plan?',
   ['Chewing is more comfortable, intake meets the agreed targets, and the weight trend stabilizes','Use clinical and intake trends rather than a single meal or weight measurement.'],
   ['The dentures are repaired but the patient still cannot eat enough','The cause may be partly addressed, but nutritional goals remain unmet.'],
   ['One supplement is taken while the rest of the day’s intake is negligible','A single product does not establish total adequacy.'],
   ['Weight appears unchanged once despite ongoing minimal intake','A single value can miss continuing risk; reassess the trend and intake.']],
  reasoning:['Why should oral and nutritional care be coordinated?',
   ['An eating barrier can prevent the patient from using an otherwise adequate meal plan','Treating the mouth and adapting intake together addresses cause and consequence.'],
   ['Dental treatment automatically corrects every nutritional deficit instantly','Recovery still requires adequate intake and monitoring.'],
   ['Weight loss proves that tube feeding is the only option','The appropriate route depends on intake, swallowing, gastrointestinal function, and the clinical assessment.'],
   ['Food texture alone determines all energy and protein needs','Texture affects access; nutrient requirements and actual intake must also be considered.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-malnutrition-03':{
  source:nutrition,scenario:'A patient needs an assessed texture-modified diet but leaves meals untouched because the available foods conflict with customary preferences. There is no plan for acceptable alternatives.',
  recognition:['Which barrier is most directly limiting intake?',
   ['The prescribed texture has not been combined with acceptable food choices','A safe texture and culturally or personally acceptable meals can often be planned together. Explore the specific concerns.'],
   ['A refusal of all care established by one untouched tray','A stated preference barrier does not prove an informed refusal of nutrition or treatment.'],
   ['Proof that the swallowing recommendations are unnecessary','Food preference does not by itself change swallowing safety. Review rather than discard the assessed texture.'],
   ['A need to impose the same menu on every patient','Individual preferences affect whether a nutrition plan is usable.']],
  action:['What should the nurse do?',
   ['Ask about acceptable foods and coordinate safe-texture alternatives with dietetic and swallowing support','Maintain the assessed safety requirements while improving acceptability and nutritional adequacy.'],
   ['Offer an unassessed regular texture simply because it is preferred','Preference matters, but changes must account for the documented swallowing risk.'],
   ['Withhold all meals until the patient accepts the standard tray','This can worsen intake and does not resolve the barrier.'],
   ['Record refusal and stop assessing the reason for poor intake','A useful record includes the stated barrier and the plan to address it.']],
  teaching:['Which explanation best supports shared planning?',
   ['We can look for foods you accept that still meet the assessed texture and nutrition needs','The patient’s preferences should inform a feasible safe plan.'],
   ['Your preferences cannot be considered once a texture is prescribed','Texture requirements do not eliminate person-centered food selection.'],
   ['A preferred food is automatically safe to swallow in any form','Preparation and swallowing assessment still matter.'],
   ['One accepted drink replaces every nutritional requirement','Total energy, protein, fluid, and micronutrient intake still need review.']],
  safety:['Which proposed approach should the nurse challenge?',
   ['Abandon the assessed texture without review to increase meal acceptance','An unsafe change can create aspiration or choking risk. Seek acceptable options within the reviewed plan.'],
   ['Monitor what is actually eaten and drunk','Offered food is not the same as consumed nutrition.'],
   ['Ask the patient which foods and preparation methods are acceptable','Specific preferences help the team design practical alternatives.'],
   ['Reassess the plan if intake remains inadequate despite adaptation','Further support or a different strategy may be needed.']],
  planning:['Which goal best addresses the problem?',
   ['Achieve adequate accepted intake using the appropriate texture and individualized support','The plan must be both safe and usable, with agreed intake monitoring.'],
   ['Deliver the standard tray regardless of whether anything is consumed','Delivery is a process measure, not evidence of adequate nutrition.'],
   ['Prioritize appearance alone without assessing texture or nutrients','Presentation can help, but safety and adequacy remain necessary.'],
   ['Label the patient nonadherent before attempting any adaptation','This omits the documented barrier and potential solutions.']],
  evaluation:['Which observation best supports improvement?',
   ['The patient eats the agreed acceptable texture-modified meals and meets the monitored intake targets','Actual consumption with preserved safety supports benefit.'],
   ['The tray is culturally appropriate but the texture no longer matches the assessed needs','Acceptance alone does not establish swallowing safety.'],
   ['The prescribed texture is correct but almost all food is still left','Safety requirements alone have not resolved inadequate intake.'],
   ['The menu is revised but no intake is measured','A plan change needs outcome reassessment.']],
  reasoning:['Why should preference be addressed alongside texture?',
   ['A technically suitable meal provides little nutrition if the patient cannot accept and consume it','Person-centered adaptation improves feasibility while safety and nutrient needs remain explicit.'],
   ['Preference removes all swallowing risk','The risks depend on the clinical assessment and food properties, not preference alone.'],
   ['Every patient from one culture has identical food choices','Ask the individual rather than assuming preferences from group identity.'],
   ['Adequate intake can be inferred from the number of trays delivered','Consumption and the overall clinical response must be assessed.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-malnutrition-02-R2':{
  source:ngSafety,scenario:'Before the first feed through a newly inserted nasogastric tube, a trainee injects air and hears a “whoosh” over the stomach. No validated placement confirmation has been documented.',
  recognition:['What is the key safety problem?',
   ['The tube position has not been confirmed by an accepted method','Auscultation after air insufflation cannot reliably exclude respiratory placement. Do not use the tube on that basis.'],
   ['The whoosh confirms that the tip is definitely in the stomach','Sound can be misleading and is not an accepted placement test.'],
   ['Absence of coughing proves the tube is correctly positioned','A misplaced tube may not provoke an obvious cough.'],
   ['A fixed insertion length guarantees correct anatomical placement','External length is useful for ongoing checks but does not independently establish initial placement.']],
  action:['What should the nurse do before any feed, fluid, or medicine is given?',
   ['Withhold tube use and obtain documented confirmation using the approved pH or radiographic pathway','Follow the local validated protocol and ensure any required X-ray is interpreted by a competent clinician. Do not instill substances before safe confirmation.'],
   ['Begin a small test feed and watch for distress','A test feed can enter the respiratory tract and cause harm.'],
   ['Repeat the air test until the sound is louder','Repetition does not make an invalid test reliable.'],
   ['Flush with water first to make aspiration easier','Introducing fluid before confirmation can harm a patient with a misplaced tube.']],
  teaching:['Which principle should be reinforced with the trainee?',
   ['Use only the approved placement-confirmation pathway before the first use','The whoosh test, tube appearance, or lack of respiratory symptoms does not establish safe placement.'],
   ['A louder sound means the position is more certain','Sound intensity is not a valid anatomical confirmation.'],
   ['Any aspirate appearance is enough without the prescribed assessment','Visual appearance alone is not an accepted initial confirmation.'],
   ['A chest X-ray can be accepted without checking that it was interpreted and documented','Radiographic confirmation requires a competent interpretation and a clear documented decision that the tube is safe to use.']],
  safety:['Which proposed action should the nurse challenge?',
   ['Start the feed solely because the whoosh was heard','This relies on an unsafe placement test and risks feeding into the respiratory tract.'],
   ['Keep the tube unused until the required confirmation is complete','Withholding use prevents introduction of substances into an unconfirmed position.'],
   ['Escalate inability to obtain an acceptable aspirate result','The protocol identifies the next step, which may include radiography.'],
   ['Check that the confirmation and safe-to-use decision are recorded','Clear documentation supports safe administration by subsequent staff.']],
  planning:['Which goal best fits the immediate situation?',
   ['Confirm safe anatomical placement before the tube is used','The need for nutrition does not justify bypassing a safety-critical placement check.'],
   ['Meet the scheduled feed time regardless of placement uncertainty','Punctual feeding cannot take priority over preventing respiratory administration.'],
   ['Use a low feeding rate to compensate for an unconfirmed position','Even a small volume in the respiratory tract can cause harm.'],
   ['Assume the next nurse will verify placement after the first feed','Confirmation is required before use, not after.']],
  evaluation:['Which finding best supports that the initial safety requirement was met?',
   ['An accepted placement assessment is documented with a clear decision that the tube is safe to use','The finding must meet the actual protocol; complete the ongoing checks and feeding plan as indicated.'],
   ['The patient tolerated the first test feed without coughing','Tolerance does not retrospectively validate the initial placement method.'],
   ['The external tube length looks typical','Length alone does not confirm initial gastric placement.'],
   ['The air test is positive on two separate attempts','Repeated invalid testing remains insufficient.']],
  reasoning:['Why is the whoosh test unsafe as initial confirmation?',
   ['A transmitted sound cannot reliably distinguish gastric from respiratory placement','An accepted pH or appropriately interpreted radiographic pathway is needed before use.'],
   ['Hearing a sound means the tube is too narrow','The sound does not provide a reliable measure of tube diameter or location.'],
   ['A misplaced tube always causes immediate obvious distress','Misplacement may be clinically silent, so validated confirmation remains necessary.'],
   ['Any uncertainty can be corrected by reducing the feed concentration','The risk is anatomical misplacement; feed concentration does not resolve it.']],
 },
 'SIT-NP4-malnutrition-04-R2':{
  source:nutrition,scenario:'A patient with a high BMI has unintentionally lost substantial weight and eaten very little for several weeks. A colleague says nutrition screening is unnecessary because the patient is not thin.',
  recognition:['Which interpretation is most accurate?',
   ['A high BMI does not exclude malnutrition or nutritional risk','Weight-loss history and reduced intake matter alongside BMI. Screen and assess the whole pattern.'],
   ['Stored body fat guarantees adequate protein and micronutrients','Energy stores do not establish current nutrient adequacy or protect against every consequence of poor intake.'],
   ['Unintentional weight loss is always beneficial at a high BMI','The cause, rate, intake, and clinical context require assessment.'],
   ['Only visibly underweight patients can have refeeding problems','Prolonged poor intake and other risk factors can be important at different body sizes.']],
  action:['What should the nurse do?',
   ['Complete nutrition screening and arrange assessment of intake, weight loss, causes, and refeeding risk','Use the appropriate tool and clinical judgment. Coordinate a safe individualized nutrition plan.'],
   ['Skip screening until BMI enters the underweight range','This can miss important risk and delay support.'],
   ['Start unrestricted high-volume feeding without assessing the prolonged low intake','Refeeding risk and metabolic status may require a cautious monitored plan.'],
   ['Assume the patient intentionally dieted and avoid asking about symptoms','The weight loss is documented as unintentional and deserves investigation.']],
  teaching:['Which explanation is accurate?',
   ['Nutrition risk depends on recent intake and weight change as well as body size','Explain why screening is appropriate without stigmatizing the patient’s weight.'],
   ['A high BMI means the body never needs protein replacement','Protein and other nutrients remain necessary.'],
   ['Rapid unintentional loss can be ignored if clothes fit better','Appearance does not determine clinical safety.'],
   ['Eating little for weeks makes a sudden large feed automatically safe','Refeeding complications may occur and require assessment and monitoring.']],
  safety:['Which proposed plan should the nurse challenge?',
   ['Exclude the patient from nutrition screening solely because BMI is high','This ignores the documented weight loss and prolonged inadequate intake.'],
   ['Review the time course and percentage of unintentional weight loss','These are important screening and assessment data.'],
   ['Assess electrolyte and refeeding concerns before rapid nutritional escalation','The plan should reflect the actual risk and clinical condition.'],
   ['Investigate symptoms and practical reasons for the low intake','Treatable causes and barriers can determine the most effective support.']],
  planning:['Which goal best fits this situation?',
   ['Restore adequate nutrition safely while addressing the cause of the unintentional decline','Use an individualized route, prescription, monitoring, and functional goals.'],
   ['Pursue further rapid weight loss before considering nutrient needs','The current unintentional decline requires clinical assessment, not automatic intensification.'],
   ['Judge nutritional recovery only by whether BMI remains high','BMI alone can conceal ongoing loss or poor intake.'],
   ['Use the same feeding rate for every patient after weeks of poor intake','Metabolic risk and requirements vary; assess before prescribing.']],
  evaluation:['Which finding best supports effective management?',
   ['Intake and relevant clinical markers improve under the monitored plan without refeeding complications','Evaluate actual intake, tolerance, electrolytes when indicated, weight trend, and function.'],
   ['BMI remains above normal while intake is still negligible','A high BMI does not show that nutritional needs are being met.'],
   ['A single weight rises during edema without assessing intake or fluid balance','Fluid gain can obscure nutritional interpretation. Review the full clinical picture.'],
   ['The patient receives a menu but cannot eat because the original barrier remains','Provision alone does not establish consumption or recovery.']],
  reasoning:['Why is BMI alone insufficient for this assessment?',
   ['It does not describe recent nutrient intake, involuntary loss, or the full clinical risk','A patient can remain at a high BMI despite clinically important nutritional deterioration.'],
   ['BMI directly measures every vitamin and protein store','BMI is a weight-for-height measure, not a complete nutritional assay.'],
   ['Only weight change matters and BMI should never be recorded','Use BMI with intake, weight trajectory, symptoms, and the clinical assessment.'],
   ['A high BMI prevents electrolyte shifts during refeeding','Refeeding risk depends on the broader history and metabolic state, not protection from body size alone.']],
 },
});

